WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.262 --> 00:00:12.150
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.632 --> 00:00:17.090
SHANE GLASS: Good afternoon,
and welcome the "Cloud OnAir,"

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:19.740
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.922
We're hosting webinars
every Tuesday.

00:00:21.922 --> 00:00:22.880
My name is Shane Glass.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.770
With me today is Andrew Whitby.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:27.140
And today, we'll be talking
about the Visualize 2030

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.130
Challenge.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.220
You can ask questions at
any time on the platform.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.810
And we have Googlers
standing by to answer them.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:34.622
Let's get started.

00:00:34.622 --> 00:00:37.080
So I will say with a note first,
this is a little different

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.110
than my last live experience,
which was a 2010 baseball

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.750
game I was broadcasting.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.851
I imagine the story will
be a little different.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:49.770
So we're talking about the
Visualize 2030 Challenge today.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:53.200
It's a data-driven story to
talk about how at least two

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.630
of the UN's Sustainable
Development Goals

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:57.760
influence each other
and what actions

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.870
might be taken in order to
reach the goals set out in 2030.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.550
Let's learn more
about the challenge.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.174
We encourage you to
visit the link here

00:01:05.174 --> 00:01:07.487
on the site on the page.

00:01:07.487 --> 00:01:09.570
And I think it's really
important that we call out

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:14.230
here that this is a real
opportunity for students that

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.540
are eligible for the contest--
university students--

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.830
to have a great impact on
the improvement of the world

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:19.330
around them.

00:01:23.138 --> 00:01:26.820
The Visualize 2030 Challenge
is done in collaboration

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.760
with the World Bank, the
United Nations Foundation,

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.060
and the Global Partnership
for Sustainable Development.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.600
We're collaborating with
them, because it just gives us

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:36.797
such a broader reach.

00:01:36.797 --> 00:01:38.380
And, Andrew, I think
we'd love to hear

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:39.421
your perspective on that.

00:01:39.421 --> 00:01:41.320
ANDREW WHITBY: Yes,
that sounds like my cue.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.111
So I'm a data scientist
in Development Data

00:01:43.111 --> 00:01:45.135
Group at the World Bank.

00:01:45.135 --> 00:01:47.010
And for people who don't
know the World Bank,

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:50.910
we're a multilateral institution
with about 189 member

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.530
countries, I think.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.870
And we work in the
realm of financing

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.060
and technical assistance
for countries, mostly

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.094
low and middle-income
countries, to help them

00:01:59.094 --> 00:02:00.510
in development
projects, so things

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:03.660
like improving a health
system, or building new roads,

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.200
or maybe expanding sanitation
and electricity networks, stuff

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.500
like that.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.440
But we also work on
collecting and publishing data

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.190
about development.

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.190
And I guess that's my
role in the organization,

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:17.130
and that's why I'm here
talking to you today.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.000
So if you skip on
to the next slide,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.470
I can talk a little bit about
the United Nations' Sustainable

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:27.260
Development Goals, which is
the focus of the challenge.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.530
The story of this is kind
of interesting to talk

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:31.780
about this week,
because at the moment,

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:33.990
not far from where we're
standing here in New York,

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:37.592
the United Nations General
Assembly is meeting.

00:02:37.592 --> 00:02:39.800
And they did the same thing
as they do in every year.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.841
They did the same thing
in 2015, as it turns out,

00:02:41.841 --> 00:02:42.690
on this exact date.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.273
They passed this
resolution committing

00:02:44.273 --> 00:02:47.580
to these 17 goals for
sustainable development

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:51.150
to take place over the period
up to, in general, 2030

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:53.340
is the target date
for these goals.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.280
And you can already
see from the screen,

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.440
it's quite a broad set of things
that they're working towards.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.147
But I'll read a couple
of them in full,

00:03:00.147 --> 00:03:02.480
because I think it gives you
some sense of the ambition.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.813
You can't necessarily tell
from the little picture form.

00:03:04.813 --> 00:03:08.460
So goal 1 is to end poverty
in all its forms everywhere.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:12.210
Goal 4 is to ensure inclusive
and equitable quality education

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:15.120
and promote lifelong learning
opportunities for all.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.910
Goal 5 is to achieve
gender equality

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:20.550
and empower all women and girls.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:24.550
Goal 10 is to reduce inequality
within and among countries.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.780
Goal 14 is to conserve
and sustainably use

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.270
the oceans, seas, and marine
resources for sustainable

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:30.790
development.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:32.039
So it's not just about people.

00:03:32.039 --> 00:03:34.150
It's about the earth
environment as well.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.150
And goal 16 is to promote
peaceful and inclusive

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:39.330
societies, provide access
to justice for all,

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.790
and to build accountable
institutions at all levels.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:45.840
So yeah, it's a very
broad spread of goals.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.174
And there's kind of some
history to this that's worth

00:03:48.174 --> 00:03:50.590
keeping in mind, which is it
was a previous set of goals--

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:52.370
the Millennium
Development Goals-- that

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:54.530
was agreed on about
2000 and then carried

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:57.540
through to the
2015 point in time.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.240
And so this set
of goals is really

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:02.060
replacing a previous
set of targets--

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:04.490
many of which were achieved
and some which were not--

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:05.570
in that period.

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:07.730
But like I said, these
are mostly setting goals

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:09.702
to be achieved by 2030.

00:04:09.702 --> 00:04:11.660
And the thing, I think,
when you listen to them

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:14.076
as I read them out and when
you look at them on the screen

00:04:14.076 --> 00:04:16.370
here, they're kind of big,
lofty, general ideals.

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:17.120
SHANE GLASS: Huge ambitions.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:17.380
ANDREW WHITBY: Right.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.269
I mean, they're not things
that you can necessarily easily

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:20.990
measure.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:22.670
They're a little bit abstract.

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:24.110
And so there's a
kind of framework

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.780
under these to make that a
bit more measurable, to make

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:30.500
this thing really work, which
is that under these 17 goals,

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:31.819
there are 169 targets.

00:04:31.819 --> 00:04:33.860
I won't read all of them
out or even a selection.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:36.494
But they're more detailed,
more specific things

00:04:36.494 --> 00:04:38.660
that you can look at and
say, yes, that was achieved

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.450
or that wasn't, by the
time we reach 2030.

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:43.910
And then underneath those
targets, each of them

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:46.850
has usually between one
and three indicators.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:48.710
And that's where the
rubber hits the road.

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:52.670
You get down to these concrete
outcomes that can be measured.

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:54.500
And I can give you
some real examples

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.845
of those that might make
it a little bit clearer.

00:04:56.845 --> 00:04:58.220
So like I said,
the first goal is

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:00.664
to end poverty in all
its forms everywhere.

00:05:00.664 --> 00:05:02.830
And that has a number of
targets associated with it.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:05.720
The first of those targets
is called target 1.1, very

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.940
numerical order to these things.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:11.540
And that is, by 2030, eradicate
extreme poverty for all people

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:13.460
everywhere, currently
measured as people

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:16.130
living below $1.25 a day.

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:18.551
And that $1.25 is an
international poverty line,

00:05:18.551 --> 00:05:20.400
that is re-evaluated
from time to time.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.410
It's actually moved since then.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.682
But that target has an
indicator associated with it.

00:05:24.682 --> 00:05:26.640
And so that's what you'll
find in the data set.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:28.140
And that indicator
is the proportion

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:30.270
of the population below
the international poverty

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:33.810
line by sex, age,
employment status,

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.880
and geographic location--
that is, whether people

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:37.374
live in an urban or rural area.

00:05:37.374 --> 00:05:38.790
And so that's the
structure of how

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.677
you get from these
lofty ambitious goals

00:05:41.677 --> 00:05:43.260
down to something
that we can actually

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:46.667
take and visualize and talk
about in concrete terms.

00:05:46.667 --> 00:05:48.000
So yeah, that's the first thing.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:49.830
These things have
to be measurable.

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:52.140
And then the second thing
that you might have picked up

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:54.630
from what I was talking about
is that whereas the previous set

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:56.430
of goals we had, the Millennium
Development Goals, it

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:58.160
was very much about
country averages.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:05:59.640
It was a very
broad-brush approach

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:01.230
to looking at this stuff.

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:02.970
One of the emphases
in the SDGs--

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:04.530
the Sustainable
Development Goals--

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:05.962
is to be able to
disaggregate that

00:06:05.962 --> 00:06:07.170
and to look within countries.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:09.499
So in that indicator for
poverty that I read out,

00:06:09.499 --> 00:06:11.040
you could see that
it was broken down

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.080
by sex, and by age, and
by employment status,

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:14.840
and the urban-rural location.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.760
So you're really looking
at sub-populations

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.780
within countries and making
sure, as the SDG mantra goes,

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:21.530
that nobody is left
behind, that everybody

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:23.760
is captured in these data sets.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.320
So that's the brief
intro to the SDGs.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:28.290
SHANE GLASS: And so
just to summarize that,

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:31.930
at the high level, you
have the end poverty.

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:34.290
And when you did down into
the indicator, that's truly

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:37.407
your measurable way
of saying, here's

00:06:37.407 --> 00:06:38.490
how we're making progress.

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:39.430
Here's where we're
making progress.

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:39.990
ANDREW WHITBY: Exactly.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:42.060
In principle, we should
be able to take a country

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:43.518
and take a point
in time and attach

00:06:43.518 --> 00:06:48.180
a number to that indicator and
say, the poverty rate in Malawi

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:49.890
in 2018 is this number.

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:50.890
So that's the idea.

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:52.697
SHANE GLASS: OK,
that makes sense.

00:06:52.697 --> 00:06:54.780
ANDREW WHITBY: So then the
next question, I guess,

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:56.090
for me is, why am I here?

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:57.840
Why am I not at my
desk at the World Bank?

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:00.910
Why am I at Google talking
about this contest?

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:02.910
And I think that comes
down to this slide, which

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:04.739
is that data has power.

00:07:04.739 --> 00:07:06.280
And it hasn't always
been accessible.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.940
So thinking about how it
hasn't been accessible--

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:12.295
and this is a mea culpa, in
a way, from the World Bank--

00:07:12.295 --> 00:07:14.990
that up until fairly
recently, and certainly

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:17.780
in the pre-internet era, if you
wanted to get hold of our data,

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:20.475
such as it is, you would send
us a check for about $300.

00:07:20.475 --> 00:07:23.450
And we would send you
back a physical disk.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:25.880
And that works fine for the
libraries and universities

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:26.180
and stuff.

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:28.680
But it obviously meant that a
lot of people around the world

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.154
didn't have access to this
data and especially people

00:07:31.154 --> 00:07:32.320
from low-income backgrounds.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:34.190
For people who we're
most trying to help,

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:37.000
this data was
completely inaccessible.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:39.330
So in about 2010,
the World Bank really

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:41.040
swung behind the
open data movement.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:42.810
And so now, all
our data is online.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.640
It's free as long as there's no
privacy restrictions stopping

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:47.050
us publishing it.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:49.390
That's something that is
available to everybody.

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:52.000
And that's where this
contest comes around.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.220
This is another way of
us promoting our data,

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:57.470
getting that out to people,
and getting people to use it.

00:07:57.470 --> 00:08:00.850
And I think why
that's important,

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:03.010
I can give an example
of a World Bank project

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:04.420
that was based around data,
which is this thing called

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:05.150
Doing Business.

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:07.150
What they do is they go
from country to country,

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:08.524
and they just
collect information

00:08:08.524 --> 00:08:10.960
about how easy it is operate
a business in that country.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:13.780
So how much paperwork does
it take to start a company?

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:14.245
SHANE GLASS: How interesting.

00:08:14.245 --> 00:08:15.661
ANDREW WHITBY: Or
how long does it

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:18.160
take to get an electricity
connection set up?

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.050
How easy is it to
resolve legal disputes?

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:24.250
Those sorts of things that are
day-to-day work for a business.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.667
And this projects has been
going on for a number of years.

00:08:26.667 --> 00:08:28.499
And all they really do
is publish this data.

00:08:28.499 --> 00:08:30.390
They publish rankings
of countries and so on.

00:08:30.390 --> 00:08:32.140
But the effect that
it has is quite large.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:35.980
Because citizens, businesses,
journalists in countries

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:37.690
will look and say,
well, our country

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:39.179
is falling behind
these other neighboring

00:08:39.179 --> 00:08:40.679
countries, who are
better on this measure

00:08:40.679 --> 00:08:41.770
or this other measure.

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:44.500
And so that can promote a sort
of responsiveness of government

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.370
to try and fix some
of these problems.

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:48.536
It's not just about these
kind of business measures.

00:08:48.536 --> 00:08:52.010
The same thing happens with
measures of life expectancy,

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:54.010
or more recently, in some
high-income countries,

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:58.572
maternal mortality has been
getting worse rather than

00:08:58.572 --> 00:08:59.717
getting better.

00:08:59.717 --> 00:09:01.800
There's been a lot of great
journalistic work done

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:05.280
taking this publicly-available
data and then writing about it.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:06.790
Then citizens can
take that issue,

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:08.535
and try and do
something about it.

00:09:08.535 --> 00:09:10.160
SHANE GLASS: In a
lot of ways, what I'm

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:12.570
hearing you say is that it's
a means of accountability

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:16.500
for whether its governments or
non-governmental organizations,

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:18.050
or whoever the
appropriate body is.

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:19.560
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, exactly.

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:22.174
And so that's part of it is
really bringing about change.

00:09:22.174 --> 00:09:24.090
The other side to it
that I think is important

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:26.280
and we shouldn't forget
about is just the value

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:28.619
in individual
citizens of countries

00:09:28.619 --> 00:09:30.785
understanding what other
people in the world's lives

00:09:30.785 --> 00:09:33.494
are like, how they live.

00:09:33.494 --> 00:09:35.406
I don't know if you're
familiar with Hans

00:09:35.406 --> 00:09:37.850
Wesseling-- the late Hans
Wesseling, unfortunately.

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:39.780
He was a great data
visualization data

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:40.434
communicator.

00:09:40.434 --> 00:09:41.850
If you haven't
seen his stuff, you

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:44.570
should get on YouTube after
this session and look him up.

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:46.320
But one of the points
he consistently made

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:48.780
was that even the
best-informed people, maybe

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:50.850
people like ourselves,
about some of the issues,

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:52.850
we still know very little
about other countries.

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:54.375
People will still
routinely misjudge

00:09:54.375 --> 00:09:56.250
what the poverty rate
is in a certain country

00:09:56.250 --> 00:10:00.480
or the unemployment rate in
other places, stuff like that.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.880
So it's valuable just to have
access to this data and people

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:06.160
be able to see it and
understand the world better.

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:08.618
SHANE GLASS: You hit on a key
point there of accessibility.

00:10:08.618 --> 00:10:09.804
And that's where I come in.

00:10:09.804 --> 00:10:10.970
ANDREW WHITBY: That's right.

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:12.636
SHANE GLASS: As our
viewers saw earlier,

00:10:12.636 --> 00:10:16.180
I manage the Google Cloud
Public Dataset Program.

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:18.202
Public data sets and
open data, in general,

00:10:18.202 --> 00:10:20.410
is something that's always
been kind of near and dear

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:21.640
to my heart.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:23.620
As I worked on my
master's, I would

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:26.290
spend 10, 12 hours a week
searching for data sets,

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:28.900
looking for data sets,
trying to find something,

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:30.620
anything I could
use to work with.

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:32.870
And that's kind of how I
became passionate about this.

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:35.890
But what I found over
time is a few things

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:37.090
with public data sets.

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:39.580
One is that we'll
never truly understand.

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:42.940
We could be the leading subject
matter experts in the data.

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:45.970
We'll never truly imagine all
the possible legitimate use

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:47.300
cases of this data.

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:49.192
So by making it
open and accessible,

00:10:49.192 --> 00:10:50.650
you open it up to
a whole new world

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:53.080
of innovation and new
ideas that you might not

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:54.410
have had in the past.

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:56.950
But the other thing is-- and
you talked about this earlier--

00:10:56.950 --> 00:10:58.630
there is legitimate
costs associated

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:00.730
with creating disks
and shipping disks,

00:11:00.730 --> 00:11:04.390
but if we can host it
out, in this case, on GCP,

00:11:04.390 --> 00:11:06.689
and make it easier
for people to get to,

00:11:06.689 --> 00:11:07.980
we can become a little smarter.

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:09.440
We can become a little better.

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:14.470
And we can become a little bit
more prepared for the world

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:15.850
around us.

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:18.040
I think that's where
we've collaborated

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:21.250
together is hosting
this data in BigQuery

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:23.580
and just making it so much
easier for people to get to

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:23.790
and to use.

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:24.910
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, absolutely.

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:26.780
We're happy to see our data
spread as far and as wide

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:27.440
as it can be.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:27.820
SHANE GLASS: Absolutely.

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:29.061
And that's our goal too.

00:11:29.061 --> 00:11:31.549
ANDREW WHITBY: Right.

00:11:31.549 --> 00:11:34.090
SHANE GLASS: So I mentioned the
Cloud Public Dataset Program.

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:37.210
Just to give you a
little overview on that,

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:40.630
we work with public data
providers, like the World Bank,

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:43.450
like the National Oceanic and
Atmospheric Administration--

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:47.520
NOAA-- among others to
host valuable data sets

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:50.980
or copies of their
valuable data sets in GCP,

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:54.580
so that users can find them,
discover them, and utilize them

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:55.830
much more quickly.

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:57.910
And what we found
is that removes

00:11:57.910 --> 00:12:01.570
a level of friction or a barrier
to entry that keeps people

00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:04.030
from utilizing data and
from doing great things

00:12:04.030 --> 00:12:05.440
in the first place.

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:07.410
I talked a little bit
earlier about BigQuery.

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:10.540
There are more than 100 data
sets available in BigQuery.

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:13.450
Just passed 100 data
sets and just passed

00:12:13.450 --> 00:12:17.670
100 petabytes of data scanned in
the three years of the program.

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:19.766
So we're really
excited about that.

00:12:19.766 --> 00:12:21.140
But it's not all
structured data.

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:24.960
We do about seven petabytes of
data in Google Cloud Storage.

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:26.620
Most of that is
satellite imagery,

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:31.720
so things like the USGS Landsat
from meteorological satellites

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:32.240
as well.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:34.540
So certainly a lot of
things out there that can

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:36.380
unlock the imagination
in some ways

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:39.640
and can help, whether
it's a university student,

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:42.430
like those eligible
for this contest,

00:12:42.430 --> 00:12:45.880
or if it's a startup that
wants to experiment on this,

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:48.040
but needs access to
the data to do so.

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:51.250
They can do so, I
think, much faster.

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:52.770
Of course, all of
our data can be

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:54.510
visualized in Data
Studio, which is what

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:56.310
we're going to focus on today.

00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:58.300
For those of you
that aren't familiar,

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:01.104
Data Studio is our free
Visual Analytics product.

00:13:01.104 --> 00:13:02.520
They can connect
the data directly

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:05.040
from BigQuery, from
the user interface,

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:08.710
or from other directions,
whether it's the BigQuery data

00:13:08.710 --> 00:13:12.320
connector in Data Studio or
if it's through Google Sheets.

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:14.269
So certainly a lot of
options there as well.

00:13:14.269 --> 00:13:16.810
And don't worry, we are going
to play with Data Studio today.

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:21.755
Just a real quick
overview-- where can we

00:13:21.755 --> 00:13:22.630
find these data sets?

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:24.690
Where can we learn a
little bit more about them?

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:26.240
I encourage our
users and our viewers

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:29.064
to go onto GCP
Marketplace, where

00:13:29.064 --> 00:13:30.480
you can see on the
left-hand side,

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:32.400
there's a list of
data sets there.

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:35.170
And that'll give you a
high-level overview of the data

00:13:35.170 --> 00:13:38.240
sets, some information
about the source, something

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:41.939
like where it came from and
who's the provider of it,

00:13:41.939 --> 00:13:44.480
as well as some sample queries
and some sample demonstrations

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:48.480
to help you get started
as quickly as possible.

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:50.060
OK, so this is the fun part.

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:52.870
Andrew, can you tell us a
little bit about the World Data

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:54.020
Indicators?

00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:56.660
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, the
World Development Indicators.

00:13:56.660 --> 00:13:57.470
I can.

00:13:57.470 --> 00:13:58.594
It's not unstructured data.

00:13:58.594 --> 00:13:59.790
It's not huge.

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:02.780
It's not petabytes like the
satellite data might be.

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:06.230
In storage size terms, it's a
fairly small, structured data

00:14:06.230 --> 00:14:07.126
set.

00:14:07.126 --> 00:14:08.750
But in terms of
development indicators,

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:11.149
it's relatively large.

00:14:11.149 --> 00:14:13.690
It's produced by the group that
I'm working with at the Bank.

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:17.240
It's been going for maybe
40 years now, I think.

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:18.940
And it's structured as a cube.

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:21.140
So you have about 1,500
development indicators

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:24.034
on different topics from health,
and education, and poverty,

00:14:24.034 --> 00:14:27.470
and government spending, and
all kinds of things like that

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:30.680
by about 200 countries--
plus or minus--

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:33.000
and by about up to 60 years.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:35.255
So the earliest date we have,
generally, is about 1960

00:14:35.255 --> 00:14:36.380
that the collection begins.

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:39.020
And some data sets start
later that that, because it

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:42.272
wasn't collected back then.

00:14:42.272 --> 00:14:44.730
I should say, also, that this
is available in lots of ways,

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:46.150
not just through your platform.

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:47.150
SHANE GLASS: Absolutely.

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:48.230
ANDREW WHITBY: So people
can go to our website,

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:49.790
and you can access it directly.

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:52.021
You can download a
bulk csv download

00:14:52.021 --> 00:14:54.520
if you just want the whole thing
in one go on your computer.

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:56.120
You can access an
API that we have

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:59.249
to be able to use it
programmatically or through R

00:14:59.249 --> 00:15:03.082
or Python, tools like that.

00:15:03.082 --> 00:15:04.540
And so you might
be wondering, what

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:06.165
is the connection
between this data set

00:15:06.165 --> 00:15:07.910
and the Sustainable
Development Goals

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:09.119
that I talked about earlier?

00:15:09.119 --> 00:15:10.910
And if you were listening
really carefully,

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:11.770
you might have heard
me say, well, there

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:13.300
are 1,500 indicators in this.

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:15.580
And the SDGs had
about 300 indicators

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:17.170
underneath those goals.

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:19.750
So one thing, obviously, is this
is a much more comprehensive

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:21.186
data set.

00:15:21.186 --> 00:15:23.560
And that's probably because
it's been around for a while,

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:25.976
so it's collected a lot of
different indicators over time.

00:15:25.976 --> 00:15:28.281
But also, it's supposed
to be more comprehensive.

00:15:28.281 --> 00:15:30.030
Whereas, the SDG's,
everything is supposed

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:33.805
to relate to a target and
this kind of structured way

00:15:33.805 --> 00:15:36.910
of thinking about
data, WDI is much more

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:38.749
a way of just
understanding the world

00:15:38.749 --> 00:15:40.681
and understanding how
other countries look

00:15:40.681 --> 00:15:42.780
at development measures.

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:44.491
So it gives you an
example of things

00:15:44.491 --> 00:15:46.449
that you might find in
here that don't directly

00:15:46.449 --> 00:15:49.190
connect to the SDGs, but
might still be interesting.

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:51.810
You'll find something like
life expectancy in here.

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:55.630
And there's no SDG target
on life expectancy.

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:59.460
But what there is in
SDG goals is the target,

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.740
for instance 3.3,
which is on reducing

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:03.087
the burden of HIV/AIDS.

00:16:03.087 --> 00:16:04.920
And so if you're trying
to think about that,

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:06.628
it might be useful to
actually understand

00:16:06.628 --> 00:16:09.064
the context of life expectancy
in different countries

00:16:09.064 --> 00:16:09.980
to make sense of that.

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:12.210
I think we may see an
example of that later on.

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:13.800
SHANE GLASS: Spoiler
alert-- we will.

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:15.510
ANDREW WHITBY: Spoiler alert.

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:17.470
But another example
would be population.

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:19.920
So there's no population
targets for either the world

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:22.701
or for countries in the
Sustainable Development Goals.

00:16:22.701 --> 00:16:24.200
But there is, for
instance, a target

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:27.680
around improving housing
and upgrading slums.

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:30.000
But obviously, that's intimately
related to population.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:31.875
So it's useful to have
these other indicators

00:16:31.875 --> 00:16:34.207
alongside the SDG
indicators to be

00:16:34.207 --> 00:16:36.540
able to make sense of the
world and tell a better story,

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:37.120
I think.

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:39.078
SHANE GLASS: And having
that historical context

00:16:39.078 --> 00:16:43.251
helps you understand, OK, we
had the Millennium Development

00:16:43.251 --> 00:16:43.750
Goals.

00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:47.390
And now, we have the
Sustainable Development Goals.

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:51.734
It helps you understand how
did we get where we are today,

00:16:51.734 --> 00:16:54.150
and what's been done in the
past that maybe has or has not

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:54.930
worked.

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:57.404
It helps you better
understand how to tell a story

00:16:57.404 --> 00:16:59.070
and what's truly
happening in the world.

00:16:59.070 --> 00:17:00.390
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, exactly.

00:17:00.390 --> 00:17:02.109
The data landscape
reflects that, right?

00:17:02.109 --> 00:17:03.650
When I say this
started 40 years ago,

00:17:03.650 --> 00:17:05.774
it didn't start with 1,500
indicators 40 years ago.

00:17:05.774 --> 00:17:08.429
It started with two, probably,
and it's grown since.

00:17:08.429 --> 00:17:09.970
The last thing I
should say about WDI

00:17:09.970 --> 00:17:12.599
is, I'm standing here
talking about it.

00:17:12.599 --> 00:17:14.450
But it's not just the
World Bank's work.

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:17.609
We collect data from a lot
of partners from UN agencies,

00:17:17.609 --> 00:17:19.670
from country governments,
from private companies.

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:22.128
And they really deserve the
credit for collecting the data.

00:17:22.128 --> 00:17:24.534
We just collate it and make
it more easily available.

00:17:24.534 --> 00:17:26.450
SHANE GLASS: So it's
truly is an effort, then,

00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:28.349
from the global community
to come together.

00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:28.890
ANDREW WHITBY: It is, yeah.

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:30.556
And now, you're hosting
on your platform

00:17:30.556 --> 00:17:32.940
is an extension of that to
make it even more available.

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:33.390
SHANE GLASS: Excellent.

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:35.848
All right, well, let's dig into
the data here a little bit.

00:17:35.848 --> 00:17:37.820
And let's dig into some
of the visualizations.

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:38.653
So we'll start here.

00:17:38.653 --> 00:17:42.840
This is a basic visualization I
put together that demonstrates,

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:47.660
I believe, it's Sustainable
Development Goal 8, Indicator

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:51.210
8.1.1, which looks
at the percentage

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:54.780
of the population in a given
country that have access

00:17:54.780 --> 00:17:56.380
to electricity.

00:17:56.380 --> 00:17:58.530
And so you'll see the heat
map up here on the top.

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:01.890
The color by countries, I
sometimes jokingly refer to it,

00:18:01.890 --> 00:18:06.000
kind of identifies where the
red is a larger percentage

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.900
of the population has access
to electricity-- the deep red--

00:18:08.900 --> 00:18:11.070
whereas, that blue,
it really stands out,

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:13.410
in sub-Saharan
Africa especially,

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:17.820
has a significantly lower
percentage of the population

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:19.410
with access to electricity.

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:22.230
And you can see called out there
at the bottom, the 15 or 20

00:18:22.230 --> 00:18:25.230
countries with the
lowest percentage

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:26.231
of the total population.

00:18:26.231 --> 00:18:28.021
And I think this is a
great example of what

00:18:28.021 --> 00:18:30.660
you talked about earlier about
how the different indicators

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:31.560
break down.

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:33.490
Because this is 8.1.1.

00:18:33.490 --> 00:18:35.850
This looks at the
total population.

00:18:35.850 --> 00:18:39.210
But then 8.1.2 looks
at rural populations,

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:42.635
and 8.1.3 looks at the
urban population with access

00:18:42.635 --> 00:18:43.260
to electricity.

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:46.550
So I think this is a
great example and a very

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:51.760
high-level overview of what's
possible with Data Studio.

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:54.669
And so let's dig into--
you have some examples.

00:18:54.669 --> 00:18:55.710
And I have some examples.

00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:55.890
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, exactly.

00:18:55.890 --> 00:18:57.210
SHANE GLASS: And we'll
show them side by side?

00:18:57.210 --> 00:18:59.070
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, I think so.

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:01.819
Yeah, I think that'll work.

00:19:01.819 --> 00:19:03.360
This is an example
of a visualization

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:05.395
that we made
earlier in the year.

00:19:05.395 --> 00:19:07.695
And it comes from a
print publication,

00:19:07.695 --> 00:19:10.290
the "Atlas of the Sustainable
Development Goals."

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:11.370
So it was made as a book.

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:12.300
You can find a PDF.

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:14.362
And there's also
an online version

00:19:14.362 --> 00:19:16.626
that you should be able
to find pretty easily.

00:19:16.626 --> 00:19:18.000
But the point of
that publication

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:20.220
is really trying to
tell these data stories

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:22.140
that were looking
at in this contest,

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:24.400
in our own particular way.

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:26.970
So this one is you're looking
at, here, a map of the world,

00:19:26.970 --> 00:19:30.870
obviously, and it's a choropleth
map, the kind of map where

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:33.720
each country's colored a
different intensity to show

00:19:33.720 --> 00:19:35.220
a number for the country.

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:36.930
And what it's showing
is this measure

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:39.450
of air pollution called PM 2.5.

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:41.790
And PM 2.5 relates
to target, I think,

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:45.240
11.6, which is talking about
the environmental quality

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:47.090
in cities.

00:19:47.090 --> 00:19:49.320
And PM 2.5 is
important, because it's

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:50.980
a very small size of particle.

00:19:50.980 --> 00:19:53.813
It's smaller than one-thirtieth
the size of a human hair.

00:19:53.813 --> 00:19:55.771
And it's the kind of
thing that you can inhale,

00:19:55.771 --> 00:19:57.701
and it will lodge
deeply in your lungs.

00:19:57.701 --> 00:19:58.950
And then it can cause disease.

00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:00.570
High levels of PM 2.5 are bad.

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:02.820
And there's a World Health
Organization recommendation

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:06.260
for the maximum exposure
you should face.

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:09.382
So one of the things, I
think, that the question that

00:20:09.382 --> 00:20:11.590
is raised for me that I
think is worth thinking about

00:20:11.590 --> 00:20:13.770
is, you have to be
careful around some

00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:14.850
of these indicators.

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:17.034
So with the WDI data
sets, as you've loaded up,

00:20:17.034 --> 00:20:18.822
people can find
the metadata there.

00:20:18.822 --> 00:20:21.510
And that will tell you something
about how it was created.

00:20:21.510 --> 00:20:22.884
So in this case,
this is actually

00:20:22.884 --> 00:20:26.146
measuring that PM 2.5
measure across a country,

00:20:26.146 --> 00:20:27.520
but then population
weighting it.

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:29.860
So it kind of reflects
the average exposure

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:31.901
that a person in that
country might have.

00:20:31.901 --> 00:20:34.150
And that's actually slightly
different to what the SDG

00:20:34.150 --> 00:20:36.700
target is looking at, which is
people in cities, particularly

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:40.550
urban people, across the entire
country or across the world.

00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:43.050
And maybe only half of
people live in cities.

00:20:43.050 --> 00:20:45.310
So in a sense, this is an
interesting visualization.

00:20:45.310 --> 00:20:47.260
But it's important to
bear those differences

00:20:47.260 --> 00:20:49.360
from the SDG target in mind.

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:51.610
But I think you have a version
of this as well, right?

00:20:51.610 --> 00:20:52.360
SHANE GLASS: Yeah.

00:20:52.360 --> 00:20:54.730
Before we step on to
that-- so PM 2.5 typically

00:20:54.730 --> 00:20:59.340
comes from burning things,
whether it's fuel, whether it's

00:20:59.340 --> 00:21:01.190
for cooking, for electricity.

00:21:01.190 --> 00:21:03.760
But I think some of the
areas highlighted here

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:07.390
could also be seen as coming
from natural sources as well.

00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:08.830
ANDREW WHITBY:
Yeah, that's right.

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:12.094
I'm not an expert in almost
any of these specific targets.

00:21:12.094 --> 00:21:13.010
SHANE GLASS: Nor am I.

00:21:13.010 --> 00:21:14.290
ANDREW WHITBY: But we produced
this book to try and look

00:21:14.290 --> 00:21:14.860
at the different areas.

00:21:14.860 --> 00:21:15.910
And so one of the
interesting things

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:18.370
is to dig into that metadata
and say, well, actually,

00:21:18.370 --> 00:21:19.328
what is this measuring?

00:21:19.328 --> 00:21:21.220
And it turns out that
PM 2.5 also measures

00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:22.600
a lot of wind-blown sand.

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:24.790
So if you have a city in
the middle of a desert,

00:21:24.790 --> 00:21:26.470
you may have high
levels of PM 2.5.

00:21:26.470 --> 00:21:27.910
And it's not air
pollution like we

00:21:27.910 --> 00:21:30.270
would think of factories, and
cars, and stuff like that.

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:31.829
It's natural pollution.

00:21:31.829 --> 00:21:33.370
And yet, it's still
measured by this.

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:34.700
So that's another reason
that people really

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:37.287
should look at the metadata
of things they're analyzing.

00:21:37.287 --> 00:21:39.620
SHANE GLASS: Yeah, so we'll
flip over to the Data Studio

00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:40.510
version of this.

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:42.940
I've very creatively
copy and pasted

00:21:42.940 --> 00:21:48.230
a lot of the text that
was on the World Bank's

00:21:48.230 --> 00:21:48.897
version of this.

00:21:48.897 --> 00:21:51.063
But I think one thing that's
also really interesting

00:21:51.063 --> 00:21:53.210
is you kind of tend
to see that cluster.

00:21:53.210 --> 00:21:57.830
These kind of countries that
see the biggest impact from this

00:21:57.830 --> 00:21:59.450
tend to be clustered together.

00:21:59.450 --> 00:22:01.760
And that's important,
because air pollution

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:04.722
doesn't obey the laws
of political boundaries.

00:22:04.722 --> 00:22:06.680
And so it's important,
as we mentioned earlier,

00:22:06.680 --> 00:22:09.230
for this global effort, this
global community to come

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:11.780
together to work on this.

00:22:11.780 --> 00:22:14.900
We talked about the
text that's on here.

00:22:14.900 --> 00:22:17.514
We kind of went back
and forth earlier.

00:22:17.514 --> 00:22:20.180
You mentioned the World Bank had
given a lot of thought to this.

00:22:20.180 --> 00:22:24.425
And I actually think it's really
one of most valuable things

00:22:24.425 --> 00:22:26.300
that the World Bank
brings to the table here.

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:28.880
It's not just the
creation and the data--

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:30.260
that's obviously valuable--

00:22:30.260 --> 00:22:33.726
but that expertise and that
understanding of data as well.

00:22:33.726 --> 00:22:35.600
ANDREW WHITBY: Interestingly,
the publication

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:37.640
that I've opened this
came from is entirely

00:22:37.640 --> 00:22:38.599
data story [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:38.599 --> 00:22:39.806
It's entirely visualizations.

00:22:39.806 --> 00:22:41.210
There's no paragraph
of the text.

00:22:41.210 --> 00:22:43.400
And that was something that
was hard for us to adjust to.

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:44.540
Because we're
like, well, we need

00:22:44.540 --> 00:22:46.498
to include these caveats
and these explanations

00:22:46.498 --> 00:22:48.650
that we would normally
spend a page writing about.

00:22:48.650 --> 00:22:51.209
But somehow, we need to fit
these into two sentences that

00:22:51.209 --> 00:22:52.250
fit somewhere on a chart.

00:22:52.250 --> 00:22:54.416
So that was a kind of
interesting experience for us.

00:22:54.416 --> 00:22:57.200
I think it becomes a really
powerful way of, then, simply,

00:22:57.200 --> 00:22:59.454
but at the same time,
richly explaining the story.

00:22:59.454 --> 00:23:01.370
SHANE GLASS: OK, so I
