00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:34.110
to create these
areas that stand out.

00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:35.746
Andrew, if I were
to ask you, what

00:26:35.746 --> 00:26:37.370
do you see that stands
out on this map?

00:26:37.370 --> 00:26:38.786
ANDREW WHITBY:
Yeah, like you say,

00:26:38.786 --> 00:26:40.810
the clustering of
dark areas, I think,

00:26:40.810 --> 00:26:44.520
jumps out most immediately.

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:47.460
SHANE GLASS: So we were trading
favorite data visualization

00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:51.210
professors earlier and
mine being Stephen Few, who

00:26:51.210 --> 00:26:54.780
talks about the hierarchy
of visual information,

00:26:54.780 --> 00:26:56.530
how the human brain
interprets that--

00:26:56.530 --> 00:26:58.740
color being at or near the top.

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:00.660
Size, I think, is
probably ahead of that.

00:27:00.660 --> 00:27:03.884
But this is very
deep red stands out

