and what is our story here.

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:13.056
One visualization
does not a story make.

00:29:13.056 --> 00:29:14.930
But it can tell you the
beginning of a story.

00:29:14.930 --> 00:29:16.370
And you can string
these together

00:29:16.370 --> 00:29:19.040
to tell the true
story of whether it's

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:21.950
looking at the historical
context of using the data going

00:29:21.950 --> 00:29:24.220
back 40, 50 years,
or whether it's

00:29:24.220 --> 00:29:27.490
what the situation on
the ground is today.

00:29:27.490 --> 00:29:29.772
So let's look at
another one then.

00:29:29.772 --> 00:29:31.980
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, so this
is another visualization

00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:35.052
taken from our "Atlas of
Sustainable Development Goals."

00:29:35.052 --> 00:29:36.510
And this one's kind
of interesting,

00:29:36.510 --> 00:29:41.470
because it's an unusual
take on a familiar issue.

00:29:41.470 --> 00:29:44.580
So this is relating to
target 3.3, which includes

00:29:44.580 --> 00:29:46.890
ending the AIDS epidemic.

00:29:46.890 --> 00:29:50.470
But rather than taking
an indicator on HIV/AIDS

00:29:50.470 --> 00:29:53.010
particularly, like the
prevalence rate, for instance,

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:56.200
what we're looking at
here is life expectancy.

00:29:56.200 --> 00:29:59.070
So just to sort of
quickly understand

00:29:59.070 --> 00:30:00.480
what you're looking
at, each line

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:02.451
represents the history
of life expectancy

00:30:02.451 --> 00:30:03.450
in a particular country.

00:30:03.450 --> 00:30:06.479
And we've used color to put
all the countries on there

00:30:06.479 --> 00:30:08.520
in a way that, hopefully,
isn't too overwhelming.

00:30:08.520 --> 00:30:10.228
But what's the first
thing that jumps out

00:30:10.228 --> 00:30:11.460
at you from looking at that?

00:30:11.460 --> 00:30:12.918
SHANE GLASS: So
I'm kind of looking

00:30:12.918 --> 00:30:14.987
at the holistic pattern,
everything moving up

00:30:14.987 --> 00:30:16.820
and to the right, which
is good news, right?

00:30:16.820 --> 00:30:18.726
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, exactly.

00:30:18.726 --> 00:30:19.600
That's exactly right.

00:30:19.600 --> 00:30:20.490
One of the things we
want people to see

00:30:20.490 --> 00:30:22.800
is that, well, you can
look on the left-hand end,

00:30:22.800 --> 00:30:25.950
and the lowest life expectancy
that a country had in 1960

00:30:25.950 --> 00:30:31.680
was 28, which is a pretty
young age for life expectancy.

00:30:31.680 --> 00:30:34.390
By 2016, that's now up to 51,
the lowest country in a data

00:30:34.390 --> 00:30:34.890
set.

00:30:34.890 --> 00:30:38.070
So that's a general
improvement across time.

00:30:38.070 --> 00:30:40.970
But I said that this was a data
visualization about HIV/AIDS.

00:30:40.970 --> 00:30:44.790
And so now we can
dig in and say, well,

00:30:44.790 --> 00:30:47.070
why is that what we're showing?

00:30:47.070 --> 00:30:48.582
And so what we've
done here-- we've

00:30:48.582 --> 00:30:50.040
highlighted in
yellow the countries

00:30:50.040 --> 00:30:51.485
in sub-Saharan Africa.

00:30:51.485 --> 00:30:55.240
That's the World Bank region
that was most affected by,

00:30:55.240 --> 00:30:57.322
or has been most
affected by AIDS.

00:30:57.322 --> 00:30:58.780
But then on top of
that, we've also

00:30:58.780 --> 00:31:02.380
highlighted in a slightly
thicker line there,

00:31:02.380 --> 00:31:05.530
bolded countries, countries
that had the highest prevalence

00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:07.298
of HIV/AIDS over that period.

00:31:07.298 --> 00:31:09.980
I think there's 10 countries
highlighted there maybe.

00:31:09.980 --> 00:31:12.660
And what you see from that
is that those countries,

00:31:12.660 --> 00:31:14.410
the life expectancy
was increasing in line

00:31:14.410 --> 00:31:15.650
with all the other countries.

00:31:15.650 --> 00:31:17.355
But then sometime
around the mid-90s

00:31:17.355 --> 00:31:18.730
through to the
mid-2000s, they've

00:31:18.730 --> 00:31:19.771
actually been decreasing.

00:31:19.771 --> 00:31:22.540
And I find that
fascinating, because it's

00:31:22.540 --> 00:31:26.950
a reflection of this
awful epidemic in terms

00:31:26.950 --> 00:31:30.401
of dry, demographic data
that you might not otherwise

00:31:30.401 --> 00:31:31.976
look at that way.

00:31:31.976 --> 00:31:33.850
On the plus side, you
can see that from about

00:31:33.850 --> 00:31:36.310
the mid-2000s onwards,
these life expectancy lines

00:31:36.310 --> 00:31:39.280
start to tick up again
as treatment gets better,

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:42.549
as prevention gets better,
as awareness increases.

00:31:42.549 --> 00:31:44.090
SHANE GLASS: Yeah,
it looks like they

00:31:44.090 --> 00:31:45.787
were able to recover
most of that loss

00:31:45.787 --> 00:31:47.620
that they saw in the
late '90s, early-2000s.

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:49.106
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, maybe.

00:31:49.106 --> 00:31:50.980
SHANE GLASS: Yeah, so
I think we'll flip over

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:53.360
to Data Studio version.

00:31:53.360 --> 00:31:56.240
We talked earlier about digging
in at a very granular level

00:31:56.240 --> 00:31:56.890
to this data.

00:31:56.890 --> 00:32:00.550
And you can see, I've chosen
to go with obscurity by color

00:32:00.550 --> 00:32:03.214
to kind of move the context
behind it by using size.

00:32:03.214 --> 00:32:04.630
We talked about
earlier about size

00:32:04.630 --> 00:32:07.780
being such a big
hierarchical importance

00:32:07.780 --> 00:32:10.340
for how your brain interprets
these visualizations.

00:32:10.340 --> 00:32:13.510
And I've chosen to call out
two countries in particular--

00:32:13.510 --> 00:32:16.920
one being Rwanda, and
the other being Zambia--

00:32:16.920 --> 00:32:19.627
by using the size, by increasing
the thickness of these lines.

00:32:19.627 --> 00:32:21.460
And you can see the
exact same pattern here.

00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:23.340
Now, what's really
interesting about this

00:32:23.340 --> 00:32:24.940
is you can mouse over this.

00:32:24.940 --> 00:32:27.880
And you can see, hey,
here is exactly what

00:32:27.880 --> 00:32:29.932
the data was at a given point.

00:32:29.932 --> 00:32:31.390
So if I come back
to, for instance,

00:32:31.390 --> 00:32:35.740
1997, like we called out, OK,
I can see the Zambian life

00:32:35.740 --> 00:32:37.250
expectancy of 43 years.

00:32:37.250 --> 00:32:44.380
But if I come out here to 2016
or 2015, it's back up to 61--

00:32:44.380 --> 00:32:50.180
closer to or even above
where it was in 1977 at 51.

00:32:50.180 --> 00:32:51.880
And so I think this
is another great way

00:32:51.880 --> 00:32:54.010
that some of the
features in Data Studio

00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:56.170
can help the students
out there participating

00:32:56.170 --> 00:32:58.690
in this challenge
really understand

00:32:58.690 --> 00:33:01.487
what's happening here and really
show what's happening here.

00:33:01.487 --> 00:33:02.320
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah.

00:33:02.320 --> 00:33:06.010
We've explored lots of
different interactive tools.

00:33:06.010 --> 00:33:10.150
And we haven't really done
an awful lot of that stuff.

00:33:10.150 --> 00:33:12.287
But it's a limitation
of what we've

00:33:12.287 --> 00:33:13.870
done in terms of
static visualizations

00:33:13.870 --> 00:33:15.620
that you can't see
individual data points.

00:33:15.620 --> 00:33:16.614
You get general trend.

00:33:16.614 --> 00:33:17.780
SHANE GLASS: Right, exactly.

00:33:17.780 --> 00:33:20.000
And that's one thing I like
about the interactivity

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:20.500
of this.

00:33:20.500 --> 00:33:24.430
It's that ability to really,
again, uncover that story

00:33:24.430 --> 00:33:27.164
that you're going to tell.

00:33:27.164 --> 00:33:28.705
Again, we talked
about adding context

00:33:28.705 --> 00:33:29.880
with text on here earlier.

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:34.460
I think the World Bank has
done a great job of this,

00:33:34.460 --> 00:33:36.380
of adding this context in here.

00:33:36.380 --> 00:33:39.790
But our users aren't going
to have this context when

00:33:39.790 --> 00:33:41.510
they begin to visualize.

00:33:41.510 --> 00:33:44.260
And so I like that there's
the ability to call this out

00:33:44.260 --> 00:33:47.401
and to very clearly say, hey,
we can discover our own context

00:33:47.401 --> 00:33:47.900
in here.

00:33:47.900 --> 00:33:49.816
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, I
think I was telling you

00:33:49.816 --> 00:33:51.280
before that I
suspect when we first

00:33:51.280 --> 00:33:52.760
produced our version
of this chart,

00:33:52.760 --> 00:33:54.805
we just graphed the
life expectancies.

00:33:54.805 --> 00:33:56.930
And we actually noticed
the patterns of reductions.

00:33:56.930 --> 00:33:59.150
And you look at that, and
you say, well, some countries

00:33:59.150 --> 00:34:00.990
are doing something different
from the general increase

00:34:00.990 --> 00:34:01.700
everyone else is doing.

00:34:01.700 --> 00:34:03.610
You probe into it, and
you can pretty quickly

00:34:03.610 --> 00:34:04.920
come up with the explanation.

00:34:04.920 --> 00:34:06.295
That's the kind
of roundabout way

00:34:06.295 --> 00:34:08.060
by which we have
gotten to it, I think.

00:34:08.060 --> 00:34:10.018
SHANE GLASS: As a data
analyst, I look at that,

00:34:10.018 --> 00:34:13.600
and I say, what's interesting
to me is what's different.

00:34:13.600 --> 00:34:16.566
That's really what calls out
to me is the general pattern

00:34:16.566 --> 00:34:18.190
move up and to the
right is great news,

00:34:18.190 --> 00:34:20.520
and is of importance,
and should be examined.

00:34:20.520 --> 00:34:22.270
But really digging
into telling that story

00:34:22.270 --> 00:34:23.560
is, what's different?

00:34:23.560 --> 00:34:24.520
And what stands out?

00:34:24.520 --> 00:34:26.070
And what's the cause of that?

00:34:26.070 --> 00:34:28.570
That's one thing that's
really exciting for me.

00:34:28.570 --> 00:34:31.690
We talked a lot about using
Data Studio last time.

00:34:31.690 --> 00:34:34.210
One thing I forgot to
call out is this ability

00:34:34.210 --> 00:34:38.400
to set the size of the
dashboard up front.

00:34:38.400 --> 00:34:39.699
And you can set multiple pages.

00:34:39.699 --> 00:34:42.100
I only have one per
visualization here.

00:34:42.100 --> 00:34:46.480
So don't feel the need to create
a massive, oversized single

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:47.489
page.

00:34:47.489 --> 00:34:49.420
But one thing I really
like in visualization

00:34:49.420 --> 00:34:52.270
is that ability to use the
limitations of your screen size

00:34:52.270 --> 00:34:55.480
to your advantage of creating
something that the user has

00:34:55.480 --> 00:34:57.130
to scroll down to discover.

00:34:57.130 --> 00:35:00.130
I think it helps the storyteller
communicate the message,

00:35:00.130 --> 00:35:03.679
but a controlled manner,
step users through in a way

00:35:03.679 --> 00:35:04.720
that they want to see it.

00:35:08.835 --> 00:35:10.210
I currently have
it set to what I

00:35:10.210 --> 00:35:13.180
think would be most convenient
for our users on the webinar

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:13.960
today.

00:35:13.960 --> 00:35:16.570
But maybe set the width a
little more narrow and setting

00:35:16.570 --> 00:35:18.370
the heights at 10,000 pixels.

00:35:18.370 --> 00:35:20.290
And really let the
viewer scroll through

00:35:20.290 --> 00:35:22.920
and go through that
discovery journey with you.

00:35:28.680 --> 00:35:30.650
We'll jump back to
the slides here.

00:35:30.650 --> 00:35:32.510
I think what we're
seeing here, Andrew,

00:35:32.510 --> 00:35:35.870
is a really great demonstration
of what's possible

00:35:35.870 --> 00:35:38.810
and how to get started
here in Data Studio,

00:35:38.810 --> 00:35:42.010
and how you can take
the WDI data set

00:35:42.010 --> 00:35:46.790
and really just dig into it,
and explore, and uncover stuff

00:35:46.790 --> 00:35:48.410
that maybe no one's
ever seen before.

00:35:48.410 --> 00:35:51.080
And that, to me,
is really exciting,

00:35:51.080 --> 00:35:52.580
especially for the
students that are

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:54.170
interested in having
an impact and improving

00:35:54.170 --> 00:35:54.950
the world around them.

00:35:54.950 --> 00:35:55.825
ANDREW WHITBY: Right.

00:35:55.825 --> 00:35:58.846
We've barely scraped the surface
of the data that's in there.

00:35:58.846 --> 00:36:00.970
I think you have some other
data sets as well there

00:36:00.970 --> 00:36:04.140
that people can look at and
combine to do visualizations.

00:36:04.140 --> 00:36:06.330
So hopefully, people find
some interesting things.

00:36:06.330 --> 00:36:09.670
SHANE GLASS: I think
they will, absolutely.

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:11.950
So we talked a lot about
telling the story today.

00:36:11.950 --> 00:36:14.920
I think that narrative
came up a lot.

00:36:14.920 --> 00:36:16.750
I think what we want
students to do here

00:36:16.750 --> 00:36:18.940
is tell your friends, folks.

00:36:18.940 --> 00:36:21.100
If you are a student, dig in.

00:36:21.100 --> 00:36:23.080
Have fun visualizing
the data set.

00:36:23.080 --> 00:36:25.240
Use the hashtag #Visualize2030.

00:36:25.240 --> 00:36:27.100
Check out the website
that's here as well.

00:36:27.100 --> 00:36:27.790
And make a difference.

00:36:27.790 --> 00:36:28.290
Who knows?

00:36:28.290 --> 00:36:30.340
Maybe you'll even win $10,000.

00:36:30.340 --> 00:36:33.915
I don't know anyone that
wouldn't want to win $10,000.

00:36:33.915 --> 00:36:36.040
If you're not a student,
tell your friends who are.

00:36:36.040 --> 00:36:37.801
If you're not a
university student,

00:36:37.801 --> 00:36:39.050
tell your friends who you are.

00:36:39.050 --> 00:36:40.164
Tell your family that are.

00:36:40.164 --> 00:36:41.830
And let them know
about this opportunity

00:36:41.830 --> 00:36:44.370
that we have out
there available.

00:36:44.370 --> 00:36:46.840
Again, the deadline for
this is November 16.

00:36:46.840 --> 00:36:50.190
So I think that's coming up a
lot faster than I expected it

00:36:50.190 --> 00:36:52.095
to, given that
September's almost over.

00:36:52.095 --> 00:36:53.640
It feels like
September just started.

00:36:53.640 --> 00:36:56.172
So certainly, get out
and get cracking on this.

00:36:56.172 --> 00:36:57.630
And we're looking
forward to seeing

00:36:57.630 --> 00:36:58.755
what comes through on this.

00:36:58.755 --> 00:37:00.264
ANDREW WHITBY: Definitely.

00:37:00.264 --> 00:37:02.610
SHANE GLASS: All right, well,
that's it for us, folks.

00:37:02.610 --> 00:37:04.609
We're going to step away
for just a moment here.

00:37:04.609 --> 00:37:07.234
But stay tuned for a
live Q&amp;A. Andrew and I,

00:37:07.234 --> 00:37:08.609
we're back in less
than a minute.

00:38:18.540 --> 00:38:19.960
OK, welcome back, everyone.

00:38:19.960 --> 00:38:21.485
I'm still here with Andrew.

00:38:21.485 --> 00:38:23.610
And we've got some audience
questions coming in.

00:38:23.610 --> 00:38:25.550
So let's dive right into these.

00:38:25.550 --> 00:38:28.937
Andrew, I think this first one
is probably for both of us.

00:38:28.937 --> 00:38:31.020
But how can data be made
more accessible to people

00:38:31.020 --> 00:38:32.140
around the world?

00:38:32.140 --> 00:38:34.639
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, I think
there's a few different aspects

00:38:34.639 --> 00:38:35.560
to that.

00:38:35.560 --> 00:38:37.602
The internet has
made this just vastly

00:38:37.602 --> 00:38:38.810
different than it was before.

00:38:38.810 --> 00:38:40.070
That's the big step change.

00:38:40.070 --> 00:38:41.820
But there are other
things we think about,

00:38:41.820 --> 00:38:44.070
too, like, licensing
issues can be very

00:38:44.070 --> 00:38:46.590
restrictive in preventing people
from getting access to data

00:38:46.590 --> 00:38:48.150
they otherwise might.

00:38:48.150 --> 00:38:50.126
So we're always working
to try and negotiate

00:38:50.126 --> 00:38:52.000
better licenses with
people we get data from,

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:53.085
so we can share it.

00:38:53.085 --> 00:38:55.710
And then the other thing that I
think is worth bearing in mind,

00:38:55.710 --> 00:38:58.779
too, is the number
of people, especially

00:38:58.779 --> 00:39:01.320
in low-income countries, who
are maybe accessing the internet

00:39:01.320 --> 00:39:03.240
from mobile devices.

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:05.850
And so we're always
conscious of how long does

00:39:05.850 --> 00:39:08.760
it take to load the World Bank's
web page on a mobile device

00:39:08.760 --> 00:39:09.660
on the kind of
connection you might

00:39:09.660 --> 00:39:10.950
have in a developing country.

00:39:10.950 --> 00:39:12.277
And it's not always good.

00:39:12.277 --> 00:39:13.860
SHANE GLASS: It's
great if you produce

00:39:13.860 --> 00:39:15.040
the best website in the world.

00:39:15.040 --> 00:39:16.250
But if no one can load it,
it doesn't really help.

00:39:16.250 --> 00:39:16.730
ANDREW WHITBY: Exactly.

00:39:16.730 --> 00:39:17.220
Yeah, yeah.

00:39:17.220 --> 00:39:18.510
SHANE GLASS: And you
touched on licensing.

00:39:18.510 --> 00:39:20.350
For me, that's probably
the big one as well.

00:39:20.350 --> 00:39:22.530
One thing I see a
lot of is data sets

00:39:22.530 --> 00:39:24.030
that are not licensed at all.

00:39:24.030 --> 00:39:26.300
There's no license
attached to them.

00:39:26.300 --> 00:39:28.880
And that can almost be worse
sometimes than a restriction

00:39:28.880 --> 00:39:30.882
license, because it
will scare users off.

00:39:30.882 --> 00:39:32.340
When users come in
and say, I don't

00:39:32.340 --> 00:39:33.835
know if I'm going to get in
trouble for using this data

00:39:33.835 --> 00:39:35.040
set, I'll play it safe.

00:39:35.040 --> 00:39:36.910
I just won't use it.

00:39:36.910 --> 00:39:38.910
Looking at some of the
open-source licenses that

00:39:38.910 --> 00:39:42.280
are out there, whether it's
Apache, Creative Commons, MIT,

00:39:42.280 --> 00:39:44.660
whatever one you want
to use, attaching

00:39:44.660 --> 00:39:47.160
that open-source license to
your data set up front, I think,

00:39:47.160 --> 00:39:49.770
can really help reduce that.

00:39:49.770 --> 00:39:52.440
ANDREW WHITBY: We're as guilty
of that as the next person.

00:39:52.440 --> 00:39:53.970
We used to not do
that really well.

00:39:53.970 --> 00:39:55.410
We're doing it a
lot better from now.

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:57.118
You can usually find
World Bank data sets

00:39:57.118 --> 00:39:58.930
and the license
attached to them.

00:39:58.930 --> 00:39:59.880
SHANE GLASS: I mean, obviously,
the important part is

00:39:59.880 --> 00:40:00.960
make the data available, right?

00:40:00.960 --> 00:40:02.950
But the licensing is
kind of that next step.

00:40:02.950 --> 00:40:05.450
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, especially
if you want data journalists

00:40:05.450 --> 00:40:06.630
to use it in a publication.

00:40:06.630 --> 00:40:08.540
And now, they're going to care
about that a little bit more

00:40:08.540 --> 00:40:09.664
than maybe a student would.

00:40:09.664 --> 00:40:11.190
SHANE GLASS: Sure, absolutely.

00:40:11.190 --> 00:40:14.430
So this next one is, how can
we improve data collection

00:40:14.430 --> 00:40:17.190
to ensure representation
and accuracy, especially

00:40:17.190 --> 00:40:18.990
in developing countries?

00:40:18.990 --> 00:40:23.370
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, that's a
huge, difficult issue, I think.

00:40:23.370 --> 00:40:26.270
It depends a lot on the type
of data you're talking about.

00:40:26.270 --> 00:40:28.020
We talked a little bit
about poverty data.

00:40:28.020 --> 00:40:30.540
A lot of that
information comes from,

00:40:30.540 --> 00:40:31.957
like large-scale
survey operations

00:40:31.957 --> 00:40:33.331
that are pretty
expensive to run,

00:40:33.331 --> 00:40:35.102
that the World Bank
and our partners

00:40:35.102 --> 00:40:38.120
put a lot of money
and effort into doing.

00:40:38.120 --> 00:40:39.680
And those, you
have the advantage

00:40:39.680 --> 00:40:41.270
that you're doing
a formal survey,

00:40:41.270 --> 00:40:43.940
so you can actually make
sure that it's representative

00:40:43.940 --> 00:40:45.374
of people in the country.

00:40:45.374 --> 00:40:47.286
But not all data is like that.

00:40:47.286 --> 00:40:50.820
A lot of data that we use
today is secondary data

00:40:50.820 --> 00:40:52.985
that you have as a side
effect of other things.

00:40:52.985 --> 00:40:55.110
And so that can be much
more difficult to make sure

00:40:55.110 --> 00:40:57.160
that they're representative.

00:40:57.160 --> 00:40:59.550
You have these two
conflicting movements.

00:40:59.550 --> 00:41:02.241
On the one hand, wanting to make
sure that nobody's left behind,

00:41:02.241 --> 00:41:03.582
and that data is representative.

00:41:03.582 --> 00:41:05.430
And on the other hand, you
have a lot of big data,

00:41:05.430 --> 00:41:07.805
so people get really excited
about, like, what can we see

00:41:07.805 --> 00:41:14.724
from people's internet usage?

00:41:14.724 --> 00:41:18.572
There's been a lot of work in
development on cell phone data,

00:41:18.572 --> 00:41:23.157
monitoring what kind of patterns
you can see in that data.

00:41:23.157 --> 00:41:24.740
Academically, it's
really interesting.

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:25.714
But it has a downside.

00:41:25.714 --> 00:41:27.130
You're only
collecting information

00:41:27.130 --> 00:41:29.620
on people who have a cell
phone which in some countries,

00:41:29.620 --> 00:41:30.621
is a minority of people.

00:41:30.621 --> 00:41:31.620
SHANE GLASS: Absolutely.

00:41:31.620 --> 00:41:33.940
ANDREW WHITBY: So there's
not a simple, single answer

00:41:33.940 --> 00:41:35.273
to that question, I don't think.

00:41:35.273 --> 00:41:37.340
It depends a lot on
where it is coming from

00:41:37.340 --> 00:41:38.670
and what the topic is.

00:41:38.670 --> 00:41:40.170
SHANE GLASS: So
real quick, what are

00:41:40.170 --> 00:41:42.720
your thoughts on whether
it's representative

00:41:42.720 --> 00:41:47.540
sampling or subsampling
populations to keep costs down

00:41:47.540 --> 00:41:50.130
for those surveys you
mentioned, but still

00:41:50.130 --> 00:41:52.520
getting a potentially
representative sample

00:41:52.520 --> 00:41:53.280
of the population?

00:41:53.280 --> 00:41:54.113
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah.

00:41:54.113 --> 00:41:56.720
It's going to be
interesting, actually.

00:41:56.720 --> 00:41:59.504
We were talking before
and I was reading out

00:41:59.504 --> 00:42:00.420
the poverty indicator.

00:42:00.420 --> 00:42:03.184
And it says, proportion
of population

00:42:03.184 --> 00:42:05.850
under international poverty line
by sex, age, employment status,

00:42:05.850 --> 00:42:07.960
and geographic location.

00:42:07.960 --> 00:42:08.940
We don't have that now.

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:10.481
You can go to the
World Bank website.

00:42:10.481 --> 00:42:14.370
And you'll find poverty
as a national average,

00:42:14.370 --> 00:42:18.084
for some large countries,
that we do that kind of thing.

00:42:18.084 --> 00:42:20.250
The countries are cleverly
structuring their surveys

00:42:20.250 --> 00:42:22.057
to get a urban/rural breakdown.

00:42:22.057 --> 00:42:23.640
But I don't know
anywhere really where

00:42:23.640 --> 00:42:25.389
you can get great data
on that broken down

00:42:25.389 --> 00:42:27.330
by all of those things.

00:42:27.330 --> 00:42:30.700
It's going to take, I
think, probably resources

00:42:30.700 --> 00:42:32.850
for a start, more
investment, and maybe

00:42:32.850 --> 00:42:34.860
some clever techniques
to get that done.

00:42:34.860 --> 00:42:36.180
SHANE GLASS: And there's a
privacy issue there, too,

00:42:36.180 --> 00:42:37.580
of saying, if there's only--

00:42:37.580 --> 00:42:37.800
ANDREW WHITBY:
There is, there is.

00:42:37.800 --> 00:42:39.420
SHANE GLASS: --people
in a rural area

00:42:39.420 --> 00:42:41.690
that are males and not working,
that's another issue too.

00:42:41.690 --> 00:42:42.180
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah.

00:42:42.180 --> 00:42:44.070
I think the SDGs aren't
going to hit that,

00:42:44.070 --> 00:42:46.500
because they're not
disaggregating so much to get

00:42:46.500 --> 00:42:48.740
down to cells of 10 people.

00:42:48.740 --> 00:42:50.406
Most cases, we're
probably still talking

00:42:50.406 --> 00:42:52.822
about hundreds of thousands
of people or tens of thousands

00:42:52.822 --> 00:42:55.210
of people, the kinds
of disaggregates

00:42:55.210 --> 00:42:56.751
that we'll be using
for this purpose.

00:42:56.751 --> 00:42:59.056
But yeah, that definitely
becomes a problem

00:42:59.056 --> 00:43:00.930
the more fine-grain you get.

00:43:00.930 --> 00:43:02.042
SHANE GLASS: Absolutely.

00:43:02.042 --> 00:43:04.000
So the third one here,
from your perspectives--

00:43:04.000 --> 00:43:06.560
and I think this is
probably your perspective--

00:43:06.560 --> 00:43:08.510
how do you measure success
in data collection?

00:43:08.510 --> 00:43:09.760
What's a win for you guys?

00:43:09.760 --> 00:43:13.000
Are there any areas that
you prioritize over others?

00:43:13.000 --> 00:43:15.686
ANDREW WHITBY: Yes, the
second is easier for me

00:43:15.686 --> 00:43:18.140
to speak to than the
first part in a way.

00:43:18.140 --> 00:43:21.054
So from the World
Bank's perspective,

00:43:21.054 --> 00:43:22.720
we sort of have a
special responsibility

00:43:22.720 --> 00:43:27.410
for goals 1 and 10, the poverty
goal and the inequality goal.

00:43:27.410 --> 00:43:30.390
The World Bank's role, at
least in recent history,

00:43:30.390 --> 00:43:33.205
has been to work on
poverty particularly.

00:43:33.205 --> 00:43:34.850
And inequality comes
along with that.

00:43:34.850 --> 00:43:37.369
So particularly collecting
data on those things

00:43:37.369 --> 00:43:39.160
has always been a
priority within the Bank.

00:43:39.160 --> 00:43:41.560
Although, as I said, we're
involved in other areas

00:43:41.560 --> 00:43:43.567
as well.

00:43:43.567 --> 00:43:45.650
How do you measure success
in your data collection

00:43:45.650 --> 00:43:46.480
and measurement?

00:43:46.480 --> 00:43:49.225
Getting good quality data,
getting the cleanest data you

00:43:49.225 --> 00:43:51.599
can get, I think, is important.

00:43:51.599 --> 00:43:52.640
It's kind of interesting.

00:43:52.640 --> 00:43:54.910
In my position and working
on this data set, the World

00:43:54.910 --> 00:43:59.191
Development Indicators, I'm
not at the collection stage.

00:43:59.191 --> 00:44:01.274
We're generally taking
this data from other people

00:44:01.274 --> 00:44:02.524
who have already collected it.

00:44:02.524 --> 00:44:05.750
And one thing-- you
can't tend to fix error

00:44:05.750 --> 00:44:08.150
in data once they've
reached that point.

00:44:08.150 --> 00:44:10.440
So collecting data, at
the point of collection,

00:44:10.440 --> 00:44:12.231
making sure things are
as clean as possible

00:44:12.231 --> 00:44:15.394
is, I think, one measure of
successful data collection.

00:44:15.394 --> 00:44:16.786
And there's a lot
of technologies

00:44:16.786 --> 00:44:18.178
that help with that right now.

00:44:18.178 --> 00:44:19.625
People are working
on the tablets,

00:44:19.625 --> 00:44:20.780
and doing interviews,
and collecting

00:44:20.780 --> 00:44:22.670
GPS locations live,
and stuff like that.

00:44:22.670 --> 00:44:24.628
Whereas, in the past, that
could have been a lot more error

00:44:24.628 --> 00:44:25.960
prone when you're working
with pencil and paper.

00:44:25.960 --> 00:44:28.560
SHANE GLASS: Yeah, and
especially as 5G starts to roll

00:44:28.560 --> 00:44:30.143
out internationally--
obviously, we're

00:44:30.143 --> 00:44:32.620
at the very early edge
of that-- but having

00:44:32.620 --> 00:44:34.870
that improved bandwidth on
a mobile capacity, I think,

00:44:34.870 --> 00:44:36.550
will help as well.

00:44:36.550 --> 00:44:37.580
ANDREW WHITBY: I suspect a
lot of the places they're

00:44:37.580 --> 00:44:40.038
collecting the poverty data,
they're lucky if they have 3G.

00:44:40.038 --> 00:44:41.385
SHANE GLASS: Yeah, absolutely.

00:44:41.385 --> 00:44:42.760
Again, we talked
about that level

00:44:42.760 --> 00:44:44.020
of investment and
resourcing, it's

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:45.292
necessary to make a success.

00:44:45.292 --> 00:44:45.720
ANDREW WHITBY: For sure.

00:44:45.720 --> 00:44:46.810
SHANE GLASS: So we'll give
you a chance to breathe.

00:44:46.810 --> 00:44:48.650
I'll take the first half
of this next question.

00:44:48.650 --> 00:44:48.930
ANDREW WHITBY: Go for it.

00:44:48.930 --> 00:44:49.514
Tell me.

00:44:49.514 --> 00:44:51.430
SHANE GLASS: But I'd
love to hear you jump in.

00:44:51.430 --> 00:44:52.710
What makes a good data story?

00:44:52.710 --> 00:44:55.210
And what difference is there
between a data visualization

00:44:55.210 --> 00:44:57.500
and an infographic?

00:44:57.500 --> 00:45:02.410
To me, what makes a good
story is that ability

00:45:02.410 --> 00:45:05.980
to walk the user through
the journey of discovery,

00:45:05.980 --> 00:45:09.379
of saying, at this high
level, whether it's globally,

00:45:09.379 --> 00:45:11.920
whether it's regionally, whether
it's at a continental level,

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:14.380
saying, OK, here's what
the situation looks like.

00:45:14.380 --> 00:45:16.060
But let's dig in, and
let's learn either

00:45:16.060 --> 00:45:19.060
why or how this
got to be and walk

00:45:19.060 --> 00:45:20.360
the user through that journey.

00:45:20.360 --> 00:45:24.084
And I really like that
process of people discovering

00:45:24.084 --> 00:45:26.500
throughout the process-- not
giving all the information up

00:45:26.500 --> 00:45:28.919
front, but using those
visualizations to help them

00:45:28.919 --> 00:45:30.460
understand not only
what's happening,

00:45:30.460 --> 00:45:32.454
but even why or
how it's happening.

00:45:32.454 --> 00:45:34.745
In terms of the difference
between a data visualization

00:45:34.745 --> 00:45:37.180
and an infographic-- we
were debating this earlier--

00:45:37.180 --> 00:45:39.130
to me, a data
visualization is a lot

00:45:39.130 --> 00:45:41.680
like what we looked at earlier,
where it's truly designed

00:45:41.680 --> 00:45:43.620
to communicate the
data behind it,

00:45:43.620 --> 00:45:46.810
whereas an infographic
is a little more designed

00:45:46.810 --> 00:45:49.380
to tell a narrative
or a story in there--

00:45:49.380 --> 00:45:51.562
tends to use less
data-y elements.

00:45:51.562 --> 00:45:53.270
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah,
more design elements

00:45:53.270 --> 00:45:54.070
SHANE GLASS: More
pictorial elements.

00:45:54.070 --> 00:45:55.420
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, exactly.

00:45:55.420 --> 00:45:56.740
It's interesting, too,
when I think about what

00:45:56.740 --> 00:45:57.740
makes a good data story.

00:45:57.740 --> 00:45:59.422
It depends so much
on the medium.

00:45:59.422 --> 00:46:01.880
And sometimes, you can choose
the medium you're working in.

00:46:01.880 --> 00:46:02.950
But sometimes, you can't.

00:46:02.950 --> 00:46:05.770
So the "Atlas" that
I was showing--

00:46:05.770 --> 00:46:07.900
the visualizations from before--

00:46:07.900 --> 00:46:10.420
that was kind of like a
traditional publication.

00:46:10.420 --> 00:46:11.980
It was a print publication--

00:46:11.980 --> 00:46:14.650
I wish I had a copy I could
plug right now to show you--

00:46:14.650 --> 00:46:16.540
but also as a PDF.

00:46:16.540 --> 00:46:18.249
It was online as an
afterthought to that.

00:46:18.249 --> 00:46:19.998
And you end up with a
really different way

00:46:19.998 --> 00:46:21.940
of telling the story
than if you start

00:46:21.940 --> 00:46:23.470
from an interactive mindset.

00:46:23.470 --> 00:46:25.470
Some of the stuff you're
seeing out of newsrooms

00:46:25.470 --> 00:46:27.440
now on interactives,
they're just

00:46:27.440 --> 00:46:29.802
really mind-blowing and amazing
because they're starting

00:46:29.802 --> 00:46:31.010
to put it in perspective now.

00:46:31.010 --> 00:46:33.160
They know that they
have a certain capacity

00:46:33.160 --> 00:46:35.660
and a certain attention span
from users and stuff like that.

00:46:35.660 --> 00:46:36.580
SHANE GLASS: Right.

00:46:36.580 --> 00:46:39.770
And we talked earlier about
some of our favorite professors

00:46:39.770 --> 00:46:42.890
for data visualization, whether
it's Stephen Few or Edward

00:46:42.890 --> 00:46:43.640
Tufte or whomever.

00:46:43.640 --> 00:46:45.884
I encourage our viewers
to go out and really

00:46:45.884 --> 00:46:46.800
dig into that as well.

00:46:46.800 --> 00:46:49.130
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, definitely,
and data journalists as well.

00:46:49.130 --> 00:46:51.220
The stuff "'The New York
Times" newsroom does is pretty

00:46:51.220 --> 00:46:51.720
amazing.

00:46:51.720 --> 00:46:53.270
SHANE GLASS: Pretty
impressive, yeah.

00:46:53.270 --> 00:46:55.400
So how can data
analytics be applied

00:46:55.400 --> 00:46:57.090
to social and
environmental issues?

00:46:57.090 --> 00:46:58.250
I'll give an example.

00:46:58.250 --> 00:47:02.840
Before I came over to Google,
I was working with NOAA

00:47:02.840 --> 00:47:04.610
to make their data,
very similar to this,

00:47:04.610 --> 00:47:06.395
more publicly
accessible and usable.

00:47:06.395 --> 00:47:08.770
And what we found is they had
produced some weather radar

00:47:08.770 --> 00:47:10.540
data as part of their mission.

00:47:10.540 --> 00:47:13.325
And they had made the
data available publicly.

00:47:13.325 --> 00:47:16.645
It's available on GCP as
well as other sources.

00:47:16.645 --> 00:47:18.710
And the strangest thing came in.

00:47:18.710 --> 00:47:21.110
A group of ornithologists--

00:47:21.110 --> 00:47:23.930
scientists studying
birds-- came in and said,

00:47:23.930 --> 00:47:26.840
I can use that data to study
bird migration patterns.

00:47:26.840 --> 00:47:31.490
And what's mind-blowing is
that to a meteorologist, that's

00:47:31.490 --> 00:47:32.822
the noise in your signal.

00:47:32.822 --> 00:47:34.530
But what's the noise
to the meterologist,

00:47:34.530 --> 00:47:37.340
for the ornithologist
is the signal.

00:47:37.340 --> 00:47:38.810
And the weather is the noise.

00:47:38.810 --> 00:47:42.200
So, again, we talked about
applying data analytics

00:47:42.200 --> 00:47:43.850
to social and
environmental issues.

00:47:43.850 --> 00:47:46.475
I think the first step is making
that data available for people

00:47:46.475 --> 00:47:49.970
to use, licensing it
properly, and then using

00:47:49.970 --> 00:47:52.970
that to help gain that
understanding of the

00:47:52.970 --> 00:47:55.610
how and why and what's
happening behind it,

00:47:55.610 --> 00:47:57.680
and using that to communicate.

00:47:57.680 --> 00:48:00.500
And I love the idea of a
data-driven application

00:48:00.500 --> 00:48:02.330
of resources.

00:48:02.330 --> 00:48:05.360
We talk about this a lot-- that
resourcing is not infinite.

00:48:05.360 --> 00:48:08.510
And so using data to make an
informed decision about that,

00:48:08.510 --> 00:48:10.010
I think, is really
critical as well.

00:48:10.010 --> 00:48:10.370
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, definitely.

00:48:10.370 --> 00:48:12.820
I think you've ticked off
some of the main things there.

00:48:12.820 --> 00:48:13.820
SHANE GLASS: So
the last one here--

00:48:13.820 --> 00:48:15.680
and I'm really glad
this question came in--

00:48:15.680 --> 00:48:17.484
I forgot to mention it, but--

00:48:17.484 --> 00:48:19.400
many fields in the data
sets use abbreviations

00:48:19.400 --> 00:48:21.020
to store information.

00:48:21.020 --> 00:48:23.710
Where can I find information
regarding those fields along

00:48:23.710 --> 00:48:25.940
with the meaning of the
abbreviated data inside them?

00:48:25.940 --> 00:48:27.030
ANDREW WHITBY: Yeah, so
that's a great question,

00:48:27.030 --> 00:48:29.030
which I will take since
it's our data that we're

00:48:29.030 --> 00:48:31.880
talking about today.

00:48:31.880 --> 00:48:35.496
Just to explain why
we do that, if you

00:48:35.496 --> 00:48:37.370
start to work with
country data at any scale,

00:48:37.370 --> 00:48:39.380
you very quickly realize
that most countries

00:48:39.380 --> 00:48:41.290
have many different names.

00:48:41.290 --> 00:48:43.646
So you might have the
United States of America.

00:48:43.646 --> 00:48:45.020
You might have
the United States.

00:48:45.020 --> 00:48:45.811
You might have USA.

00:48:45.811 --> 00:48:46.790
You might have America.

00:48:46.790 --> 00:48:48.539
All of those things
in different data sets

00:48:48.539 --> 00:48:50.964
might refer to the same country.

00:48:50.964 --> 00:48:53.630
So a lot of the times, we'll try
to use standardized conventions

00:48:53.630 --> 00:48:54.171
where we can.

00:48:54.171 --> 00:48:57.580
So for instance, we include the
ISO three-letter country codes

00:48:57.580 --> 00:49:00.415
for countries as well as
the name, the World Bank's

00:49:00.415 --> 00:49:02.540
understanding of the official
name alongside there.

00:49:05.240 --> 00:49:07.280
When I'm doing internal
manipulation of data,

00:49:07.280 --> 00:49:10.699
and joining things,
and filtering things,

00:49:10.699 --> 00:49:12.990
I try to use those codes
rather than the country names,

00:49:12.990 --> 00:49:15.949
because I don't
like the dependency.

00:49:15.949 --> 00:49:17.240
Countries change names as well.

00:49:17.240 --> 00:49:19.090
That's something you
wouldn't imagine.

00:49:19.090 --> 00:49:20.250
But you work in this
area, and suddenly, you

00:49:20.250 --> 00:49:22.291
realize, every few years,
a country will actually

00:49:22.291 --> 00:49:23.560
change its official name.

00:49:23.560 --> 00:49:25.530
SHANE GLASS: Yeah, if you
tried a join on Zaire today,

00:49:25.530 --> 00:49:26.200
you'd have some trouble.

00:49:26.200 --> 00:49:26.910
ANDREW WHITBY: Right, exactly.

00:49:26.910 --> 00:49:29.290
And those problems are
still filtering through data

00:49:29.290 --> 00:49:30.256
sets you find today.

00:49:30.256 --> 00:49:32.380
So that's the reason they
exist in the first place.

00:49:32.380 --> 00:49:35.792
To find out what they refer
to, directly, I think,

00:49:35.792 --> 00:49:37.500
as you've learned
about on your platform,

00:49:37.500 --> 00:49:39.210
there's multiple tables.

00:49:39.210 --> 00:49:41.590
There'll be a table
for, say, countries

00:49:41.590 --> 00:49:43.690
where you'll see the
connection between countries

00:49:43.690 --> 00:49:45.850
to some of the metadata
of the country.

00:49:45.850 --> 00:49:48.490
The same from series,
you'll get more information

00:49:48.490 --> 00:49:51.180
about the series itself
from a series table.

00:49:51.180 --> 00:49:52.541
So you can find that stuff.

00:49:52.541 --> 00:49:54.790
And, I guess, you can access
that through the platform

00:49:54.790 --> 00:49:55.030
as well.

00:49:55.030 --> 00:49:57.760
But if you go to our website
for World Development Indicators

00:49:57.760 --> 00:50:01.090
on the World Bank website,
you can fairly easily

00:50:01.090 --> 00:50:03.139
dig into that stuff.

00:50:03.139 --> 00:50:04.930
It's not always as
obvious as it should be.

00:50:04.930 --> 00:50:08.110
Some of those you may
have to dig down a bit.

00:50:08.110 --> 00:50:11.090
And as a last, final result,
we also have a data help desk

00:50:11.090 --> 00:50:13.747
email address, which I wish I
knew right now, but I don't.

00:50:13.747 --> 00:50:15.580
But if you Google World
Bank data help desk,

00:50:15.580 --> 00:50:17.324
I'm sure you'll
find the page that

00:50:17.324 --> 00:50:18.490
gives you the email address.

00:50:18.490 --> 00:50:20.615
And they're pretty good at
answering those queries.

00:50:20.615 --> 00:50:23.270
So if you find something you
just can't make sense of--

00:50:23.270 --> 00:50:25.436
SHANE GLASS: Yeah, we talked
about blending earlier.

00:50:25.436 --> 00:50:28.180
I think that's another that you
mentioned a great way to just

00:50:28.180 --> 00:50:32.175
explore what some of that means
and whether it makes more sense

00:50:32.175 --> 00:50:34.300
for you to have the full
name on your visualization

00:50:34.300 --> 00:50:37.010
or have some of the
abbreviated names as well.

00:50:37.010 --> 00:50:38.320
ANDREW WHITBY: Three-letter
country codes are great for me.

00:50:38.320 --> 00:50:40.150
But most people don't find
them particularly enjoying.

00:50:40.150 --> 00:50:40.710
SHANE GLASS: Sure.

00:50:40.710 --> 00:50:41.980
I feel the same way
about airport codes.

00:50:41.980 --> 00:50:43.250
ANDREW WHITBY: There you go.

00:50:43.250 --> 00:50:45.550
SHANE GLASS: OK, well,
that is it for today.

00:50:45.550 --> 00:50:48.610
Tune in next Tuesday for
the next "Cloud OnAir."

00:50:48.610 --> 00:50:49.810
Thank you for watching.

00:50:49.810 --> 00:50:51.310
ANDREW WHITBY: Thanks.

00:50:51.310 --> 00:50:54.060
[MUSIC PLAYING]

